/** One paragraph per Liu He, Liu Chong and Liu Hai pair, keyed "A|B" in cycle order. */
export const PAIR_RELATION_TEXT: Record<string, string> = {
  "Rat|Ox": "The Rat and the Ox are the first of the six Liu He pairs, sometimes called secret friends. Their branches, Zi and Chou, sit side by side at the start of the cycle, and the race story links them directly: the Ox carried the Rat across the river. Traditionally the pairing is read as a partnership of quick wits and steady strength, in which the Rat supplies ideas and the Ox supplies the persistence to carry them out.",
  "Tiger|Pig": "The Tiger and the Pig form a Liu He pair, one of the six secret-friend combinations. The bold, restless Tiger and the warm, easygoing Pig are often described as balancing each other: the Tiger protects and energises, and the Pig offers kindness and a place to rest. Traditional readings see the Pig's generosity softening the Tiger's intensity, and the Tiger's courage encouraging the Pig to act.",
  "Rabbit|Dog": "The Rabbit and the Dog form a Liu He pair, one of the six secret-friend combinations. Both are described as kind and principled, the Rabbit through tact and the Dog through loyalty. Traditional readings see the Dog offering the Rabbit protection and steadiness, and the Rabbit offering the Dog gentleness and calm, which can ease the Dog's tendency to worry.",
  "Dragon|Rooster": "The Dragon and the Rooster form a Liu He pair, one of the six secret-friend combinations. The Dragon brings vision and charisma and the Rooster brings precision and organisation, so the pairing is often read as ambition meeting execution. Both have pride and high standards, which can be a shared language as long as each respects the other's strengths.",
  "Snake|Monkey": "The Snake and the Monkey form a Liu He pair, one of the six secret-friend combinations. Both are clever, the Snake through patient insight and the Monkey through quick invention. Traditional readings see this as a meeting of minds in which strategy and improvisation complement each other, with a caution that two clever animals can also outmanoeuvre each other if trust is thin.",
  "Horse|Goat": "The Horse and the Goat form the last of the six Liu He pairs, and their branches, Wu and Wei, sit side by side at the height of summer. The energetic Horse and the gentle Goat are often described as a nurturing match: the Horse brings drive and protection and the Goat brings warmth, creativity and a sense of home to return to.",
  "Rat|Horse": "The Rat and the Horse are a Liu Chong pair, one of the six clashes. Their branches sit directly opposite each other on the twelve-animal wheel, Zi at midnight and the depths of winter, Wu at noon and midsummer. Traditional readings describe the pairing as a meeting of opposites: the Rat is cautious and strategic, the Horse spontaneous and freedom-loving. The clash points to strong attraction and strong friction, especially around planning versus spontaneity.",
  "Ox|Goat": "The Ox and the Goat are a Liu Chong pair, one of the six clashes, sitting opposite each other on the wheel. Both have Earth as their fixed element, yet they are usually described as having very different temperaments: the Ox steady and practical, the Goat sensitive and imaginative. Traditional readings point to friction between duty and feeling, which can turn into balance when each values what the other offers.",
  "Tiger|Monkey": "The Tiger and the Monkey are a Liu Chong pair, one of the six clashes, opposite each other on the wheel. The Tiger leads with force and conviction and the Monkey with cleverness and play, so traditional readings describe a lively but competitive pairing in which each can provoke the other. Respect for different kinds of strength is usually named as the key.",
  "Rabbit|Rooster": "The Rabbit and the Rooster are a Liu Chong pair, one of the six clashes, sitting opposite each other at the spring and autumn points of the wheel. The gentle, conflict-averse Rabbit and the frank, exacting Rooster are often described as rubbing each other the wrong way: the Rooster's candour can wound the Rabbit, and the Rabbit's avoidance can frustrate the Rooster.",
  "Dragon|Dog": "The Dragon and the Dog are a Liu Chong pair, one of the six clashes. Both have Earth as their fixed element and sit opposite each other on the wheel. Traditional readings contrast the Dragon's grand ambitions with the Dog's practical principles, a pairing in which idealism and realism can either check each other usefully or lock into argument.",
  "Snake|Pig": "The Snake and the Pig are a Liu Chong pair, one of the six clashes, opposite each other on the wheel. Their fixed elements, Fire and Water, are themselves a classic opposition. Traditional readings contrast the Snake's guarded, strategic nature with the Pig's open, trusting one, so the pairing can struggle over trust and transparency unless both make deliberate effort.",
  "Rat|Goat": "The Rat and the Goat form a Liu Hai pair, one of the six harms. Liu Hai pairings are traditionally read as relationships where misunderstandings and small grievances accumulate rather than erupt. The practical Rat and the sensitive Goat may misread each other's needs, the Rat seeing the Goat as dependent and the Goat seeing the Rat as calculating, unless they talk openly.",
  "Ox|Horse": "The Ox and the Horse form a Liu Hai pair, one of the six harms. The steady, patient Ox and the fast, restless Horse are often described as moving at different speeds, which can lead to frustration and quiet resentment rather than open conflict. Traditional readings suggest that shared goals and explicit agreements about pace help this pairing.",
  "Tiger|Snake": "The Tiger and the Snake form a Liu Hai pair, one of the six harms. The direct, impulsive Tiger and the reserved, calculating Snake can struggle to read each other: the Tiger may find the Snake secretive, and the Snake may find the Tiger reckless. Traditional readings point to trust and communication as the central work.",
  "Rabbit|Dragon": "The Rabbit and the Dragon form a Liu Hai pair, one of the six harms. The gentle, cautious Rabbit and the bold, commanding Dragon are often described as having different ideas about how much space each should take up. The Rabbit can feel overshadowed and the Dragon underappreciated unless both make room for the other's style.",
  "Monkey|Pig": "The Monkey and the Pig form a Liu Hai pair, one of the six harms. The clever, restless Monkey and the trusting, easygoing Pig may fall into a pattern where one feels outwitted and the other feels held back. Traditional readings suggest honesty and fairness as the safeguards for this pairing.",
  "Rooster|Dog": "The Rooster and the Dog form a Liu Hai pair, one of the six harms, and sit side by side on the wheel in autumn. Both are principled and outspoken, so criticism can travel in both directions and wound more than intended. Traditional readings suggest that shared values, gently expressed, help this pairing more than winning arguments.",
};

